Q: Spring boot / Jackson 3 registering ValueDeserializerModifier and ValueSerializerModifier A: <p>I hit almost the same issue, took a while to figure out. In my case, I was trying to register a custom serializer in Spring boot 4, while upgrading from 3.5, and what used to work in boot 3.5 with Jackson 2, stopped working in boot 4 with Jackson 3.</p>
<p><strong>The issue (most likely a bug in Spring):</strong></p>
<p>Declaring a bean <code>JsonMapperBuilderCustomizer</code> to register a Jackson module, or directly a bean of type <code>JacksonModule</code> doesn't work, not entirely. These configurations do get picked up, but only gets applied to <code>ProjectingJacksonHttpMessageConverter</code>. Spring boot 4 has <code>JacksonHttpMessageConverter</code> which gets instantiated during startup, and was responsible for JSON serialization in my case. The issue was that <code>ObjectMapper</code>, which in Jackson 3 becomes <code>JsonMapper</code> is now an immutable class, and there was no direct approach available to override the &quot;building&quot; phase of the <code>JsonMapper</code> when <code>JacksonHttpMessageConverter</code> is being constructed. The reason I think it is a bug is that someone took the effort to scan for <code>JacksonModule</code> and include it for <code>ProjectingJacksonHttpMessageConverter</code>, but not for <code>JacksonHttpMessageConverter</code>.</p>
<p><strong>The solution:</strong></p>
<p>Include a <code>@Configuration</code> subclassing from <code>WebMvcConfigurer</code> and override <code>configureMessageConverters</code> which is new method introduced in Spring boot 4.</p>
<pre><code>    @Override
    public void configureMessageConverters(ServerBuilder builder) {
        builder.configureMessageConverters(converter -&gt; {
            if (converter instanceof JacksonJsonHttpMessageConverter jacksonConverter) {

                JsonMapper jsonMapper = jacksonConverter.getMapper().rebuild()
                        .addModule(myBaseJacksonModule())
                        .build();

                jacksonConverter.registerMappersForType(BaseModel.class, mapMediaType2JsonMapper -&gt; {
                    mapMediaType2JsonMapper.put(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON, jsonMapper);
                });

            }
        });
    }

/* 
* You could try just using SimpleModule to add the serializer/deserializer, including another approach which helped me while debugging the framework
*/
    JacksonModule myBaseJacksonModule() {
        return new JacksonModule() {
            @Override
            public Version version() {
                return new Version(1, 0, 0, null, null, null);
            }

            @Override
            public String getModuleName() {
                return &quot;my-base-module&quot;;
            }

            @Override
            public void setupModule(SetupContext context) {
                context.addSerializers(new Serializers.Base() {

                    @Override
                    public ValueSerializer&lt;?&gt; findSerializer(SerializationConfig config, JavaType type, Supplier beanDescRef, Value formatOverrides) {

                        if (BaseModel.class.isAssignableFrom(type.getRawClass())) { 
                            return new MyBaseSerializer(); 
                        }

                        return super.findSerializer(config, type, beanDescRef, formatOverrides);
                    }

                });
            }
        };
    }
</code></pre>
<p>The code needs to be trimmed a bit to create just one instance of the serializer, especially if it's a singleton and/or if you need any other Spring beans injected into it, etc., but hopefully this gets over the hurdle that got me stuck for a bit!</p>

Q: How can we draw something between the background and the contents of an arbitrary View instance that we don&#39;t own? A: <p>Android actually has a specialized mechanism for this that was seemingly crafted
for, and is kinda half-hidden in, its ripple implementation: <code>RenderNode</code>
projection. (Note that using this class requires hardware-acceleration, and it's
not available in the SDK until API level 29.)</p>
<p><a href="https://developer.android.com/reference/android/graphics/RenderNode" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>RenderNode</code></a>s have been a fundamental component of the <code>View</code>
framework since Lollipop, though not publicly accessible on older versions.
They're essentially containers for groups of primitive graphics operations that
can be recorded, manipulated, and replayed together as singular units composed
in tree hierarchies, greatly simplifying and improving draw routines. They
provide a standard <code>Canvas</code> for the draw, and offer various common methods with
which to apply matrix and filter operations.</p>
<p>They also have two unique methods:
<a href="https://developer.android.com/reference/android/graphics/RenderNode#setProjectBackwards(boolean)" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>setProjectBackwards()</code></a> and
<a href="https://developer.android.com/reference/android/graphics/RenderNode#setProjectionReceiver(boolean)" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>setProjectionReceiver()</code></a>, which work together to move
a node's draw up the tree a step. Have a look at the ASCII diagram and its
description from <code>setProjectBackwards()</code>:</p>
<blockquote>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>        +-------------+
        |RenderNode: P|
        +-+----------++
          |          |
          v          v
  +-------+-----+  +-+--------------+
  |RenderNode: C|  |RenderNode: P'BG|
  +-------+-----+  +----------------+
          |
          |
 +--------+-------+
 |RenderNode: C'BG|
 +----------------+
</code></pre>
<p>If P'BG is a projection receiver, and C'BG is set to project backwards then
C'BG will behave as if it was drawn directly by P'BG instead of by C.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>…which is, of course, crystal clear. I'll try to clarify: the unintuitive
structure is due to framework internals. Each <code>View</code> actually manages two
<code>RenderNode</code>s: one base node for the complete draw, and a second, nested one
that handles only the background drawable. The above diagram is describing a
<strong>P</strong>arent <code>View</code> at the top with its <strong>B</strong>ack<strong>G</strong>round below it on the right,
and a <strong>C</strong>hild <code>View</code> to the left with its own <strong>BG</strong> underneath. Put simply,
projection allows us to draw directly on the parent's background from a child's.</p>
<h3>Example</h3>
<p>We can exploit this mechanism to draw on a target's background from its own
overlay by mimicking a child <code>View</code>'s node setup there. (I generally avoid using
the foreground because it has unexpected, daresay broken, bounds behavior,
though that wouldn't necessarily be a deal-breaker here.) The background will
already be set up as a receiver, so we just need to provide the projector:</p>
<pre class="lang-kotlin prettyprint-override"><code>import android.graphics.Canvas
import android.graphics.ColorFilter
import android.graphics.PixelFormat
import android.graphics.Rect
import android.graphics.RenderNode
import android.graphics.drawable.Drawable
import android.view.View
import androidx.annotation.RequiresApi

@RequiresApi(29)
class OverlayProjector(
    private val target: View,
    private val onDraw: (Canvas) -&gt; Unit
) : Drawable() {

    private val base = RenderNode(null)

    private val projector =
        RenderNode(null).apply { setProjectBackwards(true) }

    private val resize =
        View.OnLayoutChangeListener { _, l, t, r, b, _, _, _, _ -&gt;
            setBounds(0, 0, r - l, b - t)
        }

    init {
        target.overlay.add(this)
        target.addOnLayoutChangeListener(resize)
        setBounds(0, 0, target.width, target.height)
    }

    fun dispose() {
        target.overlay.remove(this)
        target.removeOnLayoutChangeListener(resize)

        base.discardDisplayList()
        projector.discardDisplayList()
    }

    override fun onBoundsChange(bounds: Rect) {
        base.setPosition(0, 0, bounds.width(), bounds.height())
        projector.setPosition(0, 0, bounds.width(), bounds.height())
    }

    override fun draw(canvas: Canvas) {
        projector.record { onDraw(it) }
        base.record { it.drawRenderNode(projector) }
        canvas.drawRenderNode(base)
    }

    @Suppress(&quot;OVERRIDE_DEPRECATION&quot;)
    override fun getOpacity(): Int = PixelFormat.TRANSLUCENT
    override fun setAlpha(alpha: Int) {}
    override fun setColorFilter(colorFilter: ColorFilter?) {}
}

@RequiresApi(29)
inline fun RenderNode.record(block: (Canvas) -&gt; Unit) {
    val canvas = this.beginRecording()
    try {
        block(canvas)
    } finally {
        this.endRecording()
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>And if we apply that to the question's <code>TextView</code> like so:</p>
<pre class="lang-kotlin prettyprint-override"><code>val textView = …  // findViewById(), View binding, whatever.
textView.background = RuledTextViewBackgroundDrawable(textView)

val needle = &quot;voluptate velit esse cillum&quot;

val selection = Path()
val highlight = Paint().apply { color = Color.MAGENTA }

val projector = OverlayProjector(textView) { canvas -&gt;
    val layout = textView.layout
    val start = textView.text.indexOf(needle)
    layout.getSelectionPath(start, start + needle.length, selection)

    val dx = textView.compoundPaddingLeft.toFloat()
    val dy = textView.compoundPaddingTop.toFloat()
    canvas.withTranslation(dx, dy) { drawPath(selection, highlight) }
}

textView.setOnClickListener { projector.dispose() }
</code></pre>
<p>…we end up with the exact result as requested, oddly enough:</p>
<p><a href="https://i.sstatic.net/LRaDfpUd.png" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><img src="https://i.sstatic.net/LRaDfpUdm.png" alt="The question's images with and without highlighting." /></a></p>
<p>I've put the background drawable class and the example text and the layout XML
in <a href="https://gist.github.com/zed-alpha/f5e655f05bf47ce1bf1f5cf7c736c7cc" rel="nofollow noreferrer">a gist</a>, if you'd like to play around with the given setup.</p>
<h3>API levels &lt; 29</h3>
<p>As noted at the start, <code>RenderNode</code>s have existed since Lollipop, but they're
hidden from the SDK until 29. It is possible to use them on older versions
(barring runtime errors) by compiling against an <code>android.jar</code> that has had them
unhidden, or by using a stubs module that's <code>compileOnly</code> in order to achieve
the same thing manually. Further details are out of scope for the intended topic
here, but I would mention that Compose uses the stubs module approach to achieve
this exact thing for <code>RenderNode</code> (<a href="https://github.com/androidx/androidx/tree/androidx-main/compose/ui/ui-android-stubs" rel="nofollow noreferrer">source</a>).</p>
<p>Both of the preceding approaches have the same dangers as using any unpublished
API, so we need at least a fallback, if not a wholly alternate solution, should
we decide not to risk the errors at all. To that end, it is possible to use
empty <code>View</code>s for their internal <code>RenderNode</code>s, though they add redundant
overhead, but even Compose uses this technique as a surefire last resort on
older versions.</p>
<p>It's a little tricky to set this up because <code>View</code>s won't use their
<code>RenderNode</code>s to draw unless they're attached to an onscreen hierarchy, and we
have to subclass <code>ViewGroup</code> in order to be able to access the
hardware-accelerated routine. (Note that the specific configuration that follows
will not work on a hierarchy consisting of a single non-<code>ViewGroup</code> <code>View</code> –
e.g., if you were to attach an <code>ImageView</code> to <code>WindowManager</code> – because
<code>ViewPainter</code> requires a <code>ViewGroupOverlay</code>. You could really attach the painter
nearly anywhere you want, though, like in a running <code>Activity</code>.)</p>
<p>First, the <code>View</code> analog of <code>OverlayProjector</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-kotlin prettyprint-override"><code>import android.graphics.Canvas
import android.graphics.ColorFilter
import android.graphics.PixelFormat
import android.graphics.Rect
import android.graphics.drawable.Drawable
import android.view.View

class ViewOverlayProjector(
    private val target: View,
    private val onDraw: (Canvas) -&gt; Unit
) : BaseDrawable() {

    private val projector =
        object : BaseDrawable() {
            override fun isProjected(): Boolean = true
            override fun draw(canvas: Canvas) = onDraw(canvas)
        }

    private val base =
        View(target.context).apply {
            visibility = View.GONE
            background = projector
        }

    private val resize =
        View.OnLayoutChangeListener { _, l, t, r, b, _, _, _, _ -&gt;
            setBounds(0, 0, r - l, b - t)
        }

    private val painter = ViewPainter(target)

    init {
        target.overlay.add(this)
        target.addOnLayoutChangeListener(resize)
        setBounds(0, 0, target.width, target.height)

        painter.add(base)
    }

    fun dispose() {
        target.overlay.remove(this)
        target.removeOnLayoutChangeListener(resize)

        painter.remove(base)
        painter.dispose()
    }

    override fun onBoundsChange(bounds: Rect) =
        base.layout(0, 0, bounds.width(), bounds.height())

    override fun draw(canvas: Canvas) = painter.drawView(canvas, base)
}

abstract class BaseDrawable : Drawable() {
    @Suppress(&quot;OVERRIDE_DEPRECATION&quot;)
    override fun getOpacity(): Int = PixelFormat.TRANSLUCENT
    override fun setAlpha(alpha: Int) {}
    override fun setColorFilter(colorFilter: ColorFilter?) {}
}
</code></pre>
<p>And its specialized draw helper, <code>ViewPainter</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-kotlin prettyprint-override"><code>import android.annotation.SuppressLint
import android.graphics.Canvas
import android.view.View
import android.view.ViewGroup
import androidx.core.view.doOnAttach

@SuppressLint(&quot;ViewConstructor&quot;)
class ViewPainter(link: View) : ViewGroup(link.context) {

    private var root: ViewGroup? = null

    init {
        visibility = GONE

        // If you were to keep a ViewPainter as a tag on the root ViewGroup
        // (handled from an external helper function instead), you could
        // easily share a single instance through a whole hierarchy.
        link.doOnAttach {
            val root = link.rootView as? ViewGroup ?: return@doOnAttach
            root.overlay.add(this)
            this.root = root
        }
    }

    fun dispose() {
        root?.overlay?.remove(this)
    }

    fun add(view: View) {
        // Using the cheapest methods possible.
        addViewInLayout(view, -1, EmptyLayoutParams, true)
    }

    fun remove(view: View) {
        // If you share a painter on the root View as described above, this
        // should check the child count after removal and self-dispose on empty.
        removeViewInLayout(view)
    }

    fun drawView(canvas: Canvas, view: View) {
        if (!isAttachedToWindow || view.parent !== this) return
        drawChild(canvas, view, drawingTime)
    }

    // This is the bare minimum as far as overrides, since it's abstract. You
    // may wish to override other methods in ViewGroup to disable them in order
    // to minimize the possible impact from the framework's normal routines.
    // Compose does this in its View implementations, and I would suggest to
    // have a look through its source for further information.
    override fun onLayout(changed: Boolean, l: Int, t: Int, r: Int, b: Int) {}
}

private val EmptyLayoutParams = ViewGroup.LayoutParams(0, 0)
</code></pre>
<p>Directly substituting <code>ViewOverlayProjector</code> in the <code>TextView</code> usage snippet
from the previous section will yield the exact same result as the <code>RenderNode</code>
version.</p>
<h3>Notes</h3>
<ul>
<li><p>The target must have a non-null background – not necessarily opaque, mind you
– otherwise no <code>RenderNode</code> is created for it, and there's nothing onto which
to project.</p>
</li>
<li><p>This works in exactly the same manner for <code>ViewGroup</code>s too, where the children
comprise the contents. It could be useful for custom shadows, or glow effects,
or maybe some sort of child selection, integrated progress indicators, etc.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li><p>This should all be usable in Compose as well, but I've yet to think of a
genuine use for it there. This mechanism has unique integration within the
<code>View</code> framework in that each background <code>RenderNode</code> is automatically set to
be a projection receiver, so half the setup is already done and waiting for us
everywhere.</p>
<p>In Compose, no such prearrangement exists, and if we are to insert receivers
ourselves, we clearly have to have access to the relevant points anyway, and
if so, we might as well just stick the draws there instead, right? It's the
same argument even if we have the user handle the final placement. I'm pretty
sure projection was only ever created with this one niche goal – drawing to an
otherwise inaccessible point from a new spot tacked onto a long-established
routine – and it's not surprising that Compose mightn't ever manifest an
equivalent situation or obstacle.</p>
<p>In any case, I should point out that Compose provides most of the features of
<code>RenderNode</code> (and the <code>View</code> fallback) on Android through its <code>GraphicsLayer</code>
API, which is basically wrappers around the native classes. However, they're
not yet utilizing or exposing the projection methods anywhere, so it's
probably easiest to use <code>RenderNode</code>s and empty <code>View</code>s directly if you'd like
to play around with this functionality in the newer framework.</p>
</li>
</ul>
